EXT. DRY CREEKBED - HIGH NOON

Cracked mud. A burnt wagon tilts on its axle. Buzzards circle high above. A single dead cottonwood throws a thin ribbon of shade.

SHERIFF ABNER TOLLIVER, 50s, gray mustache, sits on a flat rock and cleans his revolver with a bandanna. He is in no hurry. Nearby, his deputy, PERCIVAL "PERCY" DUNMORE, 20s, paces with a rifle across his shoulder, sweating through his shirt.

Across the dry creek, a woman crouches by the wagon, sifting ash through her fingers. ESPERANZA QUILLAN, 30s, in a man's hat and a dusty duster, doesn't look up.

                    PERCY
Sheriff, we've been here an hour.

                    ABNER
Two.

                    PERCY
She hasn't moved.

                    ABNER
Neither have I.

                    PERCY
She's looking for something.

                    ABNER
Everyone is.

Percy shifts the rifle to the other shoulder. Esperanza stands, brushes her hands, and crosses the creekbed to them with an unhurried step.

                    ESPERANZA
Afternoon.

                    ABNER
Ma'am.

                    ESPERANZA
This your wagon?

                    ABNER
Used to be.

                    ESPERANZA
It's a shame.

                    ABNER
It's the cost of doing business.

                    ESPERANZA
Whose business?

                    ABNER
Mine, until Tuesday.

She nods slowly. Pulls a folded paper from her pocket, hands it to him. He reads it, his mustache twitching. Then he laughs, once, without joy.

                    ABNER (CONT'D)
Well, I'll be.

                    PERCY
What is it?

                    ABNER
Deed to the whole valley.

                    PERCY
Whose name's on it?

                    ABNER
Hers.

A new sound. Hooves in the distance. All three turn.

A rider emerges from the heat shimmer, big black horse lathered white, a rifle across his saddle. He reins in at the edge of the creekbed. SILAS DEARBORN-FLYNT, 60s, face like a hatchet, tips his hat.

                    SILAS
Sheriff.

                    ABNER
Silas.

                    SILAS
You've got my property.

                    ABNER
Do I?

                    SILAS
Her.

Esperanza steps forward, one boot at a time. She meets his eyes without flinching.

                    ESPERANZA
I'm not property.

                    SILAS
That paper says different.

                    ESPERANZA
That paper says I own what you thought you did.

Silas looks at the sheriff. The sheriff looks at the sky. A buzzard cries.

                    ABNER
Percy.

                    PERCY
Sir?

                    ABNER
Go home.

                    PERCY
But, Sheriff--

                    ABNER
Go home, son.

Percy lowers the rifle. Backs up three steps. Then turns and walks off down the creekbed, boots kicking up dust.

Silas turns the horse in a slow half circle. Spits.

                    SILAS
This isn't over.

                    ESPERANZA
It is for today.

He spurs the horse away. Dust follows him. Abner folds the deed along its old creases and hands it back, tipping his hat.
